  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few widely scattered showers will be possible \ntonight, otherwise dry weather is expected through Labor Day.\n\nOur region will continue to be sandwiched in between a stalled upper \nlow spinning over Newfoundland/Nova Scotia and a stout upper level \nridge dominating over the central CONUS through Sunday morning, \nwhile surface high pressure just south of James Bay will continue to \nbuild south into the upper Great Lakes tonight. Our area will \ncontinue to be on the western fringe of the upper low, thus anytime \na shortwave trough pinwheeling about the upper low moves over or \nnear our area, there will be at least a small chance for some \nisolated to widely scattered showers. As of early this evening...a \nfew of these are currently ongoing from the Saint Lawrence Valley \ninto central NY in association with one such passing impulse.\n\nCool advection will continue tonight with 850 mb temps getting down \naround +9C. With lake temps still sitting around +22/+23C, this will \nbe enough to elicit a weak lake response overnight. Off Lake Erie a \nconvergent NE to ENE flow will set up...with most of the activity \nexpected to remain offshore. Meanwhile a N to NNE cross-lake flow \nover Lake Ontario will also produce some lake clouds south of that \nlake, and with an added boost from upslope flow, we may see a few \nwidely scattered/scattered light lake enhanced showers develop... \nwith the best chances for these found across the Finger Lakes \nregion. Otherwise, most of the area will remain dry with comfortable \nsleeping weather. \n\nThe upper low will finally begin to pull away later Sunday and \nSunday night, while the upper level ridge axis builds a bit further \neast into the upper Great Lakes. Surface high pressure over the \nupper Great Lakes tonight will build south into the lower Great \nLakes region for Sunday and Labor Day. This will end any lingering \nshower chances, with dry weather expected for the remainder of the \nlong holiday weekend.",
